<article class="env-lemma" id="0BYB">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0BYB">Lemma <span data-tag="0BYB">53.9.1</span>.</a> Let $Z \subset \mathbf{P}^2_ k$ be a closed subscheme which is equidimensional of dimension $1$ and has no embedded points (equivalently $Z$ is Cohen-Macaulay). Then the ideal $I(Z) \subset k[T_0, T_1, T_2]$ corresponding to $Z$ is principal. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      This is a special case of Divisors, Lemma <a href="/tag/0BXL" data-tag="0BXL">31.32.3</a> (see also Varieties, Lemma <a href="/tag/0BXU" data-tag="0BXU">33.34.4</a>). The parenthetical statement follows from the fact that a $1$ dimensional Noetherian scheme is Cohen-Macaulay if and only if it has no embedded points, see Divisors, Lemma <a href="/tag/0BXG" data-tag="0BXG">31.4.4</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>